# Defect 2184, 2026-10-22, panel 227's R2: a label at a position a built-in
# does not name was accepted and read by no rule, where a declared function's
# `f(b: 1)` is `wrong_label`: `len(foo: line)` and the labels of the
# specification's own placeholders, `repeat(s: "ab", n: 3)`, checked clean.
# The sentence's bare names are values (R2), so each label here is refused,
# `wrong_label`, and dropped by a `certain` fix: the compiler reads these
# arguments by position whatever the label says, so the program the fixes
# write, `.fixed` beside this file, does what this one meant.

function main()
    line = "hello world"
    xs = [1, 2, 3]
    print(len(foo: line))  #~ wrong_label
    print(repeat(s: "ab", n: 3))  #~ wrong_label wrong_label
    print(join(xs: ["a", "b"], sep: ","))  #~ wrong_label wrong_label
    print(slice(xs: line, from: 0, to: 5))  #~ wrong_label
    print(push(xs: xs, x: 4).len())  #~ wrong_label wrong_label
    print(to_str(x: 3))  #~ wrong_label
    print(chars(of: line).len())  #~ wrong_label
    print(value: 1)  #~ wrong_label
